WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.310
Ever watched a horror movie and wondered just
how those actors manage to get through playing

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:06.379
such terrifying and disturbing roles?

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.720
Well, the truth is: some of them barely do.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:13.560
These actors have experienced everything from
abusive directors to creepy on-set experiences

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.820
to full-blown psychological breakdowns.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.300
Here are a few of the brave souls who took
on roles that ended up messing with their

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:19.800
heads.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.440
Screaming in space

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.320
The chest-bursting scene in Alien ranks among
the most iconic moments in sci-fi history.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.760
Part of what made that moment so impactful
might be that the cast involved had no idea

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.220
what was about to happen to them.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.220
Director Ridley Scott made sure that the creature’s
reveal would happen in real-time during shooting,

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.760
hoping for more authentic reactions from his
actors.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:40.740
It worked.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.060
Actress Veronica Cartwright later remembered:
"You see this thing start to come out, so

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.999
we all get sucked in, we lean forward to check
it out […] all of a sudden it comes out.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.900
I tell you, none of us expected it."

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.820
It was Cartwright herself who suffered the
most intense reaction of all to the scene

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.500
- when the blood hit her during the take,
she passed out.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:58.940
Hotel trauma

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.400
Stanley Kubrick pretty much obliterated the
the psyche of actress Shelley Duvall during

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.400
shooting on his adaptation of Stephen King's
The Shining.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.220
His mistreatment of Duvall included ordering
cast and crew not to help her when upset and

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.040
actively hindering her efforts to deliver
her performance.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:19.280
And because the whole film was shot chronologically,
the viewer is treated to an entirely realistic

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.500
portrayal of the unravelling of someone’s
mental well-being.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.140
According to Duvall: “Stanley pushed me
and prodded me further than I've ever been

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:26.800
pushed before.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.660
It's the most difficult role I've ever had
to play."

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:34.720
She had to keep bottles of water nearby on-set
to keep her hydrated after her bouts of hysterics

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.130
and even confronted Kubrick with clumps of
her own hair to demonstrate just how unwell

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.340
she was during the shoot.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.600
Perhaps worst of all, Kubrick forced her to
perform her iconic, traumatic baseball bat

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.640
scene over and over again across a stunning
127 takes.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.160
“And are you concerned about me?"

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:53.880
"Of course I am!"

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.360
"Of course you are!”

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:57.740
The Omen’s curse

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:04.090
Richard Donner's 1976 creepfest The Omen has
a rich history of freaky incidents that solidified

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.700
the antichrist-centric flick’s reputation
as one of the most cursed sets of all time

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.780
- and it was Gregory Peck who bore the brunt
of the tragedy.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.020
His son Jonathan shot himself just two months
prior to production on the movie, and then

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:20.209
his plane was struck by lightning - something
that would also go on to happen to The Omen’s

00:02:20.209 --> 00:02:21.880
executive producer Mace Neufeld.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.560
Elsewhere during production, an animal handler
would be mauled by a tiger, a special effects

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.520
assistant would be beheaded in a car crash
- near a car sign which read “Ommen, 66.6km”

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.980
- and a plane originally chartered for use
on aerial shots during shooting would go down

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.040
in Israel, killing everyone on board.

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:44.180
These eerie happenings had everyone so spooked
that producer Harvey Bernhard actually carried

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:45.380
a cross with him on set.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.400
It was Bernhard who said of The Omen: “the
devil was at work - and he didn't want that

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:49.900
film made."

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.360
Man in the mask

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.500
During shooting on The Texas Chainsaw Massacre,
Tobe Hooper withheld the appearance of Leatherface

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.260
from his actors until the cameras actually
started rolling.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.480
Gunnar Hansen, who played Leatherface, has
spoken of the difficulties endured by his

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:07.190
fellow cast members during filming on the
movie’s iconic dinner table scene.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.670
He said: "The whole dinner scene is burned
in my memory.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.620
I think just because of the misery of it.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.920
At that point we were really just on the verge
of mental collapse.”

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.799
Marilyn Burns, who played Sally, experienced
the worst of it.

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:18.379
Marilyn Burns, who played Sally, experienced
the worst of it.

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:22.299
Burns herself later told Hansen that: "I thought
you were really going to hurt me.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.950
You couldn't see through your stupid mask."

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.640
“I’ll do anything you want."

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:32.380
Lost in the woods

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.959
The Blair Witch Project was filmed over the
course of an eight-day camping trip - if you

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:37.860
can call it that.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:42.020
There was practically no interaction between
the cast and crew, save for a daily meeting

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:45.780
meant to provide the actors with supplies
and instructions on where to go next.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:49.840
The crew would then go on to stalk the actors
during the daytime, rattle their tents at

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:53.639
night when they were sleeping and, as the
shoot progressed, give them less and less

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.220
food so they’d become steadily more frustrated
and exhausted by each other.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.820
“No Mike, it's not the same log.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:02.780
It's not the same log, Mike!"

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:03.799
"Same log!"

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:04.420
"Look, it's not."

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.420
"It is, open your eyes!"

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.120
The devil’s work

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:13.779
As a child star of one of the most iconic
horror movies of all time, Linda Blair experienced

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.500
no small number of side-effects from shooting
The Exorcist.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:20.350
Despite not really understanding much about
the depravity of the movie during filming

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:24.920
- to her, it was simply acting - she was faced
with a litany of questions after its release

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.780
about possession, Catholicism and the nature
of faith.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.300
To say she was unprepared for this is something
of an understatement.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.940
She later said: "To me The Exorcist was a
work of fiction.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.319
I didn't realize then that it dealt with anything
in reality, and so when the press kept asking

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:42.020
me about all the devil stuff, it just kept
adding to the pressure I was under, and it

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.400
was just an awful thing to go through as a
teenager."

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:45.740
"Can you feel this?"

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.380
"I don't feel anything."

